- DICK CULLUM
E IS GOOD news that Andy Cohen is coming back to play in the annual Saints-Millers Old Timers Game July 16.
Andy was one of the most popular of the old Millers, He has given his life to baseball ever since.
You will have difficulty naming a player who let his head do more than Andy to extend his physical capabilities,
Watching him play, you wondered how one second baseman could know so much more than other second basemen about how to play the batter.
It took Millers’ fans a while to appreciate Andy because they seldom saw him make a spectacular play,
At last, after he had been around long enough, fans realized that he was making the play in his head before
the pitch. He was getting to or leaning toward the point where the batted ball would go.
Andy had no startling assets in speed, throwing arm or batting punch. He made his contribution by never doing anything wrong.
He frustrated opponents by killing their efforts in a an offhand way that he made their good efforts seem
eeble.
He was not a high average batter, excepting for one or two good seasons, but his ratio of run-scoring hits to
total hits was the highest.
He was also one of the Millers’ most popular men, among teammates and opponents as well as among fans.
John McGraw of the New York Giants was always on the hunt for a Jewish player to appeal to the large body of Jewish fans in New York. He passed up Hank Greenberg in one of his faulty judgments, but he gave Cohen a good try. Andy fell just short of meeting McGraw’s requirements although he had one year in which he batted .294 in 10] games. He came to the Millers after that. Through seven years of service he became one of
the fans’ best remembered heroes and one of owner-manager Mike Kelley’s favorite people.

