Giants’ Relief Specialist McMahon Will Be Starting 20th Year In Game By Tom Kane
CASA GRANDE, Ariz. — Relief specialist Don McMahon, at 40 the pitching patriarch of the Giants, will
begin his 20th season in baseball this spring.
By this time relief pitching could be called old hat as far as McMahon is concerned. Almost all of his baseball
life has been spent in the bull pen.
McMahon, who resides in Garden Grove, Calif., was assigned without warning to a relief assignment in only
his second year in the sport by his Denver manager Andy Cohen, veteran and former major league coach.
“The Denver club knew I was going to have to fulfill a military commitment in the Army during the 1951 
season,” Don stated, “and one day in the spring Andy handed me a ball
and told me to join the relief crew.
And most of the time since that is where I have been.”
McMahon was signed originally by the old Boston Braves organization and eventually helped the Milwaukee Braves to win two pennants as a rescue artist. His travels within the major leagues have taken him to Houston, Cleveland, Boston, Chicago,
Detroit and finally to San Francisco on Aug. 8, 1969.
